# Gubbins bridge — example environment file.
#
# Copy to `.env` (which is git-ignored) and fill in real values locally. NEVER commit
# a real token or a real snapshot path. Placeholders only in this file.
#
# These are consumed from Phase HA-3 onward (the HTTP server); HA-1 only uses the
# snapshot path argument passed to the CLI. Listed now so the config surface is clear.
#
# The MCP stdio server (`node mcp.mjs`) uses only GUBBINS_SNAPSHOT_PATH and
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_ALLOW_WRITES — stdio carries no credential at all, so the host/port/rate-limit
# settings below do not apply to it, and its writes are recorded against the System user.

# Absolute path to the synced gubbins-sync.json the PWA writes (NAS / synced drive).
GUBBINS_SNAPSHOT_PATH=/path/to/your/synced/gubbins-sync.json

# There is NO inbound token setting here. Callers authenticate with a per-user API token
# minted in the Gubbins app (Users -> a user -> API tokens); the bridge resolves it to that
# user and enforces their permissions on every request. Access is granted and revoked in the
# app, and takes effect as soon as the change reaches this bridge's snapshot — no .env edit
# and no restart. A bridge with no tokens minted answers 401 to everything, which is the safe
# default rather than an outage waiting to happen.

# Network bind. Default 127.0.0.1 (local only); set 0.0.0.0 to deliberately expose on LAN.
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_HOST=127.0.0.1
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_PORT=8787

# Per-client (per-IP) rate limit — a backstop against runaway query loops. A client may
# burst up to CAPACITY requests, then is held to REFILL requests/second. Defaults shown.
# Set CAPACITY=0 to disable entirely and defer to the LAN/firewall.
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_RATE_CAPACITY=60
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_RATE_REFILL=1

# Which BROWSER origins may read a bridge response cross-origin (CORS). Defaults to the
# hosted app origin (https://bootblock.github.io); loopback (a dev server) is always allowed
# on top. Only browsers are affected — Home Assistant, curl and scrapes send no Origin and
# are unaffected. Set this only if you self-host the Gubbins app on your own domain AND use
# "push to bridge" from the browser: list your origin(s), comma-separated. Set to "*" to
# restore the old permissive wildcard.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://gubbins.example.com

# Optional mDNS / zeroconf advertising so Home Assistant can auto-discover the bridge
# instead of you typing its host/port. Off by default; it carries NO secret (the bearer
# token is never advertised — you still enter it in HA). It is only meaningful when the
# bridge is LAN-exposed (GUBBINS_BRIDGE_HOST=0.0.0.0); on the loopback default it is
# auto-skipped. Set to "on" to enable.
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_MDNS=off
# Optional service instance name shown in a discovery browser (default "Gubbins Bridge").
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_MDNS_NAME=Gubbins Bridge

# Optional LIMITED WRITES. The bridge is strictly read-only by default. Set to "on" to enable
# the POST stock-adjust endpoints (check-in / check-out, quantity adjust). Each write is applied
# through the app's OWN mutation and written back into the synced gubbins-sync.json, so the PWA
# merges it conflict-free on its next sync — never a bespoke database write. Leave OFF unless you
# specifically want an automation / voice command to change stock. Same bearer token + rate limit.
# NOTE: this also enables the matching MCP stock-adjust tools, so an LLM/agent wired to the MCP
# server can change stock too. MCP runs over stdio and carries NO bearer token — its boundary is
# the OS process — so turning this on trusts whoever can launch that server.
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_ALLOW_WRITES=off

# Optional SNAPSHOT PUSH ("push to bridge"). For a user who does NOT use FS-Access sync (no shared
# folder), the PWA can POST its whole dataset straight to the bridge. Set to "on" to enable
# POST /api/v1/snapshot, which accepts the same versioned backup JSON the bridge reads and merges
# it into the served snapshot (the watcher then re-hydrates it). Off by default and a SEPARATE
# opt-in from writes above. IMPORTANT: this is NOT a lesser privilege than writes — it is WIDER. A
# write applies one bounded, reversible stock delta; a push merges caller-supplied content into the
# WHOLE dataset (any item, location, even users and permissions). Treat enabling push as at least as
# sensitive as enabling writes. JSON source only — refused for a raw .sqlite source. Same bearer
# token + rate limit; the caller's account must hold bridge:write + sync:write.
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_ALLOW_PUSH=off
# Optional hard cap (bytes) on a pushed snapshot; default 64 MiB (67108864). Lower it on a
# constrained host (e.g. a Pi/NAS on an SD card). An over-large push is rejected with HTTP 413.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_MAX_PUSH_BYTES=67108864

# Optional SNAPSHOT-STALENESS THRESHOLD. A failed snapshot reload keeps the last good data live, so
# GET /health counts consecutive failures and reports ok:false once it reaches this many in a row —
# letting a dashboard or monitor degrade instead of trusting out-of-date stock levels. Default 3
# (one failure is usually just a snapshot caught mid-write). Set to 0 to keep the counters in
# /health but never flip ok.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_STALE_AFTER_FAILURES=3

# Optional READ-ONLY EVENT STREAM (EI-1). Set to "on" to expose GET /api/v1/events — a
# Server-Sent Events feed that streams a typed event for each inventory change (item.created,
# stock.adjusted, item.low_stock, …). Same bearer token + rate limit; strictly read-only. Off by
# default (the path is a 404 when off). Enabling webhooks below implies this too.
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_EVENTS=off

# Optional READ-TRIGGERED LOOKUP EVENT. Set to "on" to also publish one "lookup.resolved" event
# each time a "where is X?" lookup resolves, carrying the query plus the matched item and LOCATION
# ids. Unlike every other event this is triggered by a READ, not an inventory change — and it
# publishes what someone SEARCHED FOR, so it is deliberately NOT implied by GUBBINS_BRIDGE_EVENTS
# and must be turned on here explicitly. It rides whichever sinks you enabled (SSE / webhooks /
# MQTT); with none enabled, nothing is sent. Off by default.
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_LOOKUP_EVENTS=off
# Window (ms) in which repeated EQUIVALENT lookups emit once — voice assistants retry and people
# rephrase, and a locate automation should not fire three times. Default 3000; 0 disables it.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_LOOKUP_EVENTS_DEBOUNCE_MS=3000

# Optional OUTBOUND WEBHOOKS. Set to "on" to deliver each event to your configured destinations.
# Targets come from TWO merged sources: the webhooks you configure in the Gubbins app (which reach
# the bridge through the snapshot it already reads, so no extra setup) and the file/env list below.
# A signed delivery carries an HMAC-SHA256 "X-Gubbins-Signature: sha256=<hex>" over the raw body
# plus a unique "X-Gubbins-Delivery" id, with bounded retry/backoff and per-target failure
# isolation. Off by default; a webhook NEVER mutates inventory. This also lights up the event stream.
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_WEBHOOKS=off
# Where the target list lives. The SECRETS live ONLY here (git-ignored) — never in a committed file.
# Default: webhooks.json beside the bridge (copy bridge/webhooks.example.json). Each target is
# { "url": "...", "secret": "...", "events": ["item.low_stock"] } — omit "events" to receive all.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_WEBHOOKS_FILE=/path/to/your/webhooks.json
# Alternative: the whole target list inline as JSON (wins over the file). Keep it in THIS .env only.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_WEBHOOKS_TARGETS=[{"url":"http://127.0.0.1:5678/webhook/gubbins","secret":"<YOUR_SIGNING_SECRET>"}]
# Named secrets that a webhook configured IN THE APP can sign with, without its secret ever being
# stored in (and synced with) your database. In the app you give the webhook a secret NAME; the
# value lives only here. This is the RECOMMENDED way to sign an app-configured webhook. It can also
# go in a "secrets" block in the webhooks file above; this env value is merged over that.
# A webhook naming a secret that is not configured here is NOT delivered — never delivered unsigned.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_WEBHOOKS_SECRETS={"my-secret-name":"<YOUR_SIGNING_SECRET>"}
# Webhook URLs are user-supplied and arrive over sync, and the bridge sits on your LAN — so by
# default it REFUSES to deliver to loopback, link-local, private and cloud-metadata addresses.
# Set this to "on" to allow them, e.g. to webhook your own Home Assistant or Node-RED on the LAN.
# Only do this if you trust every webhook URL configured in the app.
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_WEBHOOKS_ALLOW_PRIVATE=off

# Optional OUTBOUND MQTT PUBLISHING (EI-5). Set to "on" to connect OUT to your MQTT broker (the
# bridge is an MQTT *client* dialling out — it opens NO inbound port) and publish inventory state to
# retained topics plus every EI-1 event to gubbins/event/<type>. Off by default; best-effort (a broker
# outage only logs a warning — the HTTP API is unaffected). It does NOT expose the SSE HTTP endpoint.
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_MQTT=off
# The broker URL. mqtt:// (plaintext, default port 1883) or mqtts:// (TLS, default 8883). Required
# when MQTT is on. Any user:pass in the URL is ignored — use the USERNAME/PASSWORD vars below.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_MQTT_URL=mqtt://127.0.0.1:1883
# Optional broker credentials — keep them in THIS git-ignored .env only; never logged.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_MQTT_USERNAME=<YOUR_MQTT_USERNAME>
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_MQTT_PASSWORD=<YOUR_MQTT_PASSWORD>
# Optional topic prefix (default "gubbins") and client id (default "gubbins-bridge").
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_MQTT_PREFIX=gubbins
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_MQTT_CLIENT_ID=gubbins-bridge
# Optional HOME ASSISTANT MQTT DISCOVERY. Set to "on" to ALSO publish HA discovery configs so Home
# Assistant auto-creates Gubbins sensors with NO custom component (an alternative to it — pick one).
# Off by default; only meaningful when MQTT is on.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_MQTT_DISCOVERY=off
# Optional HA discovery prefix (default "homeassistant" — match HA's "discovery_prefix" if you changed it).
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_MQTT_DISCOVERY_PREFIX=homeassistant

# Optional HOME ASSISTANT READS (issue #122). Set to "on" to let the "count by weight" screen in
# the app read a live weight off a Home Assistant scale entity. The bridge calls HA as an OUTBOUND
# client (it opens NO inbound port) and can only LIST entity states and READ one — it cannot call a
# Home Assistant service, so this can never actuate anything in your home. Off by default; when off
# the /api/v1/scale/* endpoints are a 404.
GUBBINS_BRIDGE_HA=off
# Base URL of your Home Assistant instance. Required when the above is on, unless you switch the
# discovery option below on and let the bridge find it for you.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_HA_URL=http://homeassistant.local:8123
# Optional HOME ASSISTANT DISCOVERY. Set to "on" to look for Home Assistant on your LAN over mDNS
# and use the address it advertises when GUBBINS_BRIDGE_HA_URL is left unset — so you do not have
# to type it, or find out at startup that you mistyped it. Off by default, like everything else
# that touches the network. A URL you set above ALWAYS wins. Discovery finds an ADDRESS only: the
# long-lived access token below is still required, and reads stay read-only either way.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_HA_DISCOVERY=off
# A Home Assistant LONG-LIVED ACCESS TOKEN (Profile → Security → Long-lived access tokens).
# Required when the above is on. Keep it in THIS git-ignored .env only; it is never logged and is
# never sent to the app — the app only ever sees the resulting weight.
# GUBBINS_BRIDGE_HA_TOKEN=<YOUR_HOME_ASSISTANT_TOKEN>
